*.zip
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
.pytest_cache/
*.egg-info/

# LLM response cache (machine-local, regenerable)
llm_prior_cache/

# Superseded/stale outputs, kept on disk but not in git
archive/
.env

# Anchor datasets: raw data + third-party clones stay out of git (hard rule).
# The compiled envelope.yaml and hand-mapping tables under src/dynbelief/anchors/
# ARE committed (small, reviewable, cited).
data/anchors/
third_party/
# HOMER: the raw upstream dataset (12 GB) as downloaded. Same hard rule —
# the loader in src/homer/ and the small derived traces under
# data/homer_traces/ ARE tracked and rebuild from a re-downloaded copy.
HOMER/

# Profile-generated symbolic episode banks (regenerable from profiles+seed;
# the hash manifest in the bank dir pins provenance).
banks/
# IPUMS ATUS microdata: redistribution is restricted by the IPUMS terms of
# use, so the extract stays local. atus/*.py (the reader/plotter) IS tracked
# and regenerates everything from a re-downloaded extract.
atus/*.dat.gz
atus/*.dat
atus/*.xml
atus/*.cbk
# Large regenerable run artifacts: a 21-day grid's run log is ~24 MB and a
# fresh one lands on every rerun. The summaries beside them (aggregate.csv,
# provenance.json, plots, healthcheck reports) stay tracked, and
# `python -m baselines.cli run <config>` rebuilds the rest.
smoke_results/**/run_log.jsonl
smoke_results/**/questions.csv

# Regenerable per-profile anchor reports (validate_profile.py output).
profiles/manual/*.anchor_report.md
results/raw_results.csv

# The budgeted-sweep raw results: 63 MB, regenerable in ~20 s by
# `python -m beliefsim.run`. The gzipped copy beside it IS tracked.

# Re-realized household timelines under extra simulator seeds. Seed 0 is
# the canonical realization and stays tracked; seeds 1+ are byte-
# deterministic from the tracked program.yaml plus the seed (no LLM) and
# ~40 MB apiece across the set, so `python -m households.reseed --seeds
# 1 2 3 4 --spatialize` rebuilds them exactly instead of git carrying
# them. Their fleet/bake-off reports under reports/baselines/*_seed<N>
# ARE tracked — those are the results, the timelines are the inputs.
profiles/households/generated/*/*/timeline_seed[1-9]*/

# Per-row dumps from the household analysis: one row per question per
# model (absence_signal, ~1.8 M rows / 33 MB) and one per filter reset
# (perpetua_resets, ~230 k rows). The AGGREGATES beside them stay tracked
# — cells.csv.gz, perpetua_edges.csv.gz, perpetua_fallback.csv.gz,
# households.json, provenance.json, the plots and the reports — and those
# are what every table and figure is built from. To rebuild the dumps:
# `python -m baselines.household_analysis` (~24 min on 100 workers), then
# `python -m baselines.perpetua_cases` for perpetua_cases.md, which reads
# absence_signal.csv.gz.
reports/baselines/**/absence_signal.csv.gz
reports/baselines/**/perpetua_resets.csv.gz

# Rate-sweep per-rate figures: four copies of the household report's
# plots, ~3 MB a rate, rebuilt in seconds from the tracked cells.csv.gz by
# `python -m baselines.household_report --in-dir reports/baselines/rate_sweep/visits<v>`.
# The cross-rate summary, its figures, findings.md and each rate's
# markdown, cells and small aggregates stay tracked.
reports/baselines/rate_sweep/visits*/*.png
